00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,400
First, let's discuss the GPIOs.

41
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:27,640
What exactly are GPIOs?

42
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:29,280
GPIO stands for General-Purpose Input and Output

43
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:33,239
.

44
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:36,080
In fact, if we want to explain it in one sentence, GPIOs

45
00:02:33,239 --> 00:02:39,200
are the

46
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:41,680
interface between the microcontroller and the outside world.

47
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:46,800
When you want to turn an LED

48
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:49,840
on, or for example read a temperature sensor,

49
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:53,000
and bring it into the microcontroller as a voltage,

50
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:55,680
or place a push button outside

51
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,159
and apply a voltage

52
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:02,400
to the microcontroller, through these

53
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:06,560
GPIO pins you actually do this.

54
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:09,799
So GPIOs are basically

55
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:12,480
the input and output pins of your microcontroller.

56
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:15,640
​

57
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:17,640
The next step is input. If we select a GPIO

58
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:21,480
and want to configure it

59
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:26,360
as an input,

60
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:29,319
it means that you have applied an output

61
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:33,480
voltage to a microcontroller pin.

62
00:03:29,319 --> 00:03:36,360
In other words,

63
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:37,959
the voltage that you apply from outside to the

64
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:41,200
microcontroller pin

65
00:03:37,959 --> 00:03:45,760
is read by that pin.

66
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:49,040
So when a microcontroller pin is configured as an input,

67
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:52,599
you are essentially reading a

68
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:54,760
voltage, or in other words, an output signal.

69
00:03:52,599 --> 00:03:57,640
you are reading it.

70
00:03:54,760 --> 00:04:02,000
If we configure the pin as an output, or

71
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:06,040
define it as an output, you are controlling something

72
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:09,159
such as your LED, your relay,

73
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:12,319
an armature, a motor, or a stator.

74
00:04:09,159 --> 00:04:15,159
For example, you turn the LED on and off.

75
00:04:12,319 --> 00:04:17,040
In other words, you are applying the voltage

76
00:04:15,159 --> 00:04:19,680
from the microcontroller itself

77
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:23,440
to the LED, relay, or anything else.

78
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:25,240
There is one point I want to mention here.

79
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:27,000
Look, when you

80
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:30,639
turn an LED on and off,

81
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,440
its current is supplied by the

82
00:04:30,639 --> 00:04:35,720
Blue Pill itself.

83
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:37,320
In practice, an LED does not draw much current,

84
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:40,039
so we place a resistor

85
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:42,360
in the circuit to limit it

86
00:04:40,039 --> 00:04:44,120
so that our LED does not burn out. But there is another case:

87
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:47,919
you connect a rotor

88
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:50,520
or a motor to the output pins of the Blue Pill

89
00:04:47,919 --> 00:04:52,520
or the microcontroller itself.

90
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:53,919
It draws a very large current

91
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:56,759
and causes

92
00:04:53,919 --> 00:05:00,960
the microcontroller itself to burn out. So here,

93
00:04:56,759 --> 00:05:06,479
do not forget that you must use a separate power supply

94
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:08,199
to provide voltage for the rotor,

95
00:05:06,479 --> 00:05:12,479
motor, or other devices that

96
00:05:08,199 --> 00:05:15,120
draw a lot of current, so that

97
00:05:12,479 --> 00:05:18,680
your microcontroller board, or

98
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:21,400
your Blue Pill, does not burn out. Be sure to

99
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:24,680
keep this point in mind.

100
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:29,720
In this session, we also want to configure an LED

101
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:31,039
as an output and, in practice,

102
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,880
what does that mean? It means that we

103
00:05:31,039 --> 00:05:36,400
give a command from the microcontroller to apply a voltage to

104
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:40,603
the LED itself, according to the configuration we made here,

105
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:42,759
so that we can turn it on and off.

106
00:05:40,603 --> 00:05:45,440
[Sigh] Here we want to see

107
00:05:42,759 --> 00:05:47,280
how the LED itself turns on. What

108
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:50,160
do we need to make the LED turn on?

109
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:53,639
LED stands for Light-Emitting

110
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:55,919
Diode. It is a light-emitting diode that, when

111
00:05:53,639 --> 00:05:58,440
we apply voltage across it,

112
00:05:55,919 --> 00:06:01,400
current flows through it and causes

113
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:02,960
the LED to turn on. So the concept of current

114
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:05,199
shows itself here; that is,

115
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:10,280
the current that enters the LED and causes

116
00:06:05,199 --> 00:06:14,319
the LED itself to turn on is that

117
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:19,120
electrical current inside the LED.

118
00:06:14,319 --> 00:06:21,400
There is also the issue of a limiting resistor.

119
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:26,599
As you can see, the internal resistance of the LED

120
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:31,280
is very low, and perhaps if we directly

121
00:06:26,599 --> 00:06:34,240
connect the LED to the output pin,

122
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:37,800
for example, it could burn out.

123
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:40,319
So we place a resistor, for example 10 kilo-ohms,

124
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:44,280
to limit the current flowing through the LED

125
00:06:40,319 --> 00:06:47,639
so that the LED does not burn out. Keep this

126
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:51,160
in mind. And finally,

127
00:06:47,639 --> 00:06:56,560
it is the GPIOs that

128
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:59,160
determine the state of the LED:

129
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:02,599
if it is off, they turn it on, and if it is on,

130
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:02,599
they turn it off.

131
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:09,440
The logic for turning the LED on and off is zero

132
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:13,039
and one. When the state is high, it receives logic one,

133
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:15,960
or about 3.3 volts,

134
00:07:13,039 --> 00:07:19,599
across the LED and causes it to turn on.

135
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:22,960
The other logic is low,

136
00:07:19,599 --> 00:07:26,039
which is logic zero; in practice, 3.3 volts

137
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:29,000
will no longer be present here, and we connect

138
00:07:26,039 --> 00:07:32,479
the LED pin itself to ground

139
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,479
so that it turns off.

