00:15:23,839 --> 00:15:26,880
we want to configure it as a pull-up,

272
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:29,519
this section is connected to 5 volts.

273
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:32,160
The switch is arranged like this; by

274
00:15:29,519 --> 00:15:35,000
pressing this switch, we

275
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:38,920
ground it. But by default it is a pull-up, meaning

276
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,880
it is connected to 5 volts. In the pull-down mode,

277
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:43,480
if we design it

278
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:46,920
this way, the PA0 pin, or the PC13 pin

279
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:50,360
that we selected, is connected to a

280
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:52,959
pull-down resistor, and this side is grounded, meaning

281
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:56,319
it is connected to ground by default. Now, if we want

282
00:15:52,959 --> 00:15:58,480
to turn something on, in practice

283
00:15:56,319 --> 00:16:01,440
a switch must be placed in the path. When

284
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:03,199
we press that switch, 5 volts is connected

285
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:07,160
to that pin, causing the

286
00:16:03,199 --> 00:16:09,480
LED to turn on. So the concept of pull-up and

287
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:12,440
pull-down is as follows: here it says

288
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:15,319
it should be pull-up or pull-down. We say neither,

289
00:16:12,440 --> 00:16:17,480
meaning that internally we are not using

290
00:16:15,319 --> 00:16:20,160
either of these two modes, and

291
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:22,639
if at some point we want to use an external

292
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:27,920
pull-up or pull-down, we can do that outside

293
00:16:22,639 --> 00:16:31,600
the microcontroller.

294
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:31,600
Another topic is the

295
00:16:32,319 --> 00:16:37,480
mode we choose, whether it is

296
00:16:34,279 --> 00:16:40,920
push-pull or open-drain. What is

297
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:44,360
the meaning of this? We want to

298
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:46,839
see whether the mode is open-drain or push-pull.

299
00:16:44,360 --> 00:16:50,720
What does that mean? Look, when we select open-drain,

300
00:16:46,839 --> 00:16:54,319
it means that when you apply a logic one to an inverter,

301
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:57,800
its output becomes zero, and when you

302
00:16:54,319 --> 00:16:59,639
feed this zero

303
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:02,040
into a transistor, in practice that

304
00:16:59,639 --> 00:17:04,400
transistor turns off, and this section is always

305
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:07,799
open. That is why we call it open-drain.

306
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:10,520
Its drain is always open, and therefore VCC

307
00:17:07,799 --> 00:17:13,880
is connected to the other side of the pin

308
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:16,439
and turns the LED on. But push-pull is not like this.

309
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:18,760
It is made of two transistors, one PMOS

310
00:17:16,439 --> 00:17:21,720
and one NMOS. When you

311
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:24,839
apply logic one to the gate, it becomes zero here,

312
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:27,640
and when this becomes zero, the NMOS

313
00:17:24,839 --> 00:17:30,400
turns off and the PMOS turns on. When the PMOS

314
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:33,320
turns on, VCC goes to the

315
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:35,880
LED and causes it to turn on.

316
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:39,160
Conversely, if we apply zero, this part becomes one,

317
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:41,559
meaning the gate input becomes one,

318
00:17:39,160 --> 00:17:43,840
causing the PMOS to turn off and the NMOS

319
00:17:41,559 --> 00:17:45,919
—whose one side is ground and the other side

320
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:47,720
is one—turns on. When this

321
00:17:45,919 --> 00:17:50,640
transistor turns on, this becomes a short circuit

322
00:17:47,720 --> 00:17:53,400
and connects logic zero to the LED, causing it

323
00:17:50,640 --> 00:17:55,840
to turn off. So push-pull

324
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:58,679
has both logic one and logic zero,

325
00:17:55,840 --> 00:18:02,640
meaning it can turn the LED on and off. Open-drain

326
00:17:58,679 --> 00:18:05,880
only causes the circuit to be open and

327
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:08,679
causes logic one to be transferred to the output.

328
00:18:05,880 --> 00:18:11,242
So we have explained these two completely

329
00:18:08,679 --> 00:18:12,960
and clearly, including what they are and how they work.

330
00:18:11,242 --> 00:18:15,400
[Clears throat] Therefore, the mode

